# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
# The confirmed NVIDIA vGPU host driver versions, one block per version. A block starts with its
# host= line. Each line is key=value; the value runs to the end of the line and is data, never
# evaluated. Lines that start with # and empty lines are skipped.
#
# This file is the one place that holds a version's data. The driver step reads a block with
# gs_version_row (gs-driver-version.sh). The build turns the blocks into the CLI's rows
# (src/gs_versions.inc, see the Makefile). The first block is the default: its kernel tree is the
# one looked for when none is named, and hints that need one version name it.
#
# A build is confirmed only when every digest of one block matches (gs_classify). The patch tool
# (driver/*.c) holds the input and output digest of each revision in its own tables, as evidence
# for each patched site. A version whose revisions the tool does not admit by digest is never
# confirmed, whatever this file says, so a new block needs its rows there too.
#
#   host                  the host driver version, as the package and its kernel tree declare it
#   package               SHA-256 of NVIDIA's vGPU host package (.run)
#   stock_object          SHA-256 of nv-kernel.o_binary, unchanged
#   scheduler_object      ... after the scheduler revision
#   gate_object           ... after module-gate
#   patched_object        ... after vf-count: the plain build's final object
#   display_object        ... after idx39: the display build's final object
#   stock_plugin          SHA-256 of libnvidia-vgpu.so.<host>, unchanged
#   mixed_plugin          ... after plugin-mixed: the input of plugin-div4, and the installed copy
#                         of earlier releases (it is replaced, never accepted)
#   patched_plugin        ... after plugin-div4: the only copy that is installed
#   plain_offsets         the eight .text offsets, zero-based, ascending, as 0x<lowercase hex>, at
#                         which the plain build's module differs from NVIDIA's
#   second                the version of the second package (display and host CUDA builds)
#   second_grid           SHA-256 of that package as the vGPU guest driver (.run)
#   second_consumer       SHA-256 of that package as the GeForce driver (.run)
#   public                the version of NVIDIA's public kernel module source (display builds).
#                         No whole-file digest of that source is recorded, so it is an identity by
#                         version only
#   profile_plain         evidence profile of the plain build
#   profile_display       evidence profile of the display build
#   profile_display_cuda  evidence profile of the display build with host CUDA
#   owner_c, owner_h,     SHA-256 of driver-patches/fb-owner/gs-fb-owner.c, gs-fb-owner.h and
#   owner_patch           fb-owner.patch: the owner of the Ada VFs' framebuffer slots as it was
#                         reviewed for this version. A confirmed build takes no other files
#   owner_symbols         the kernel symbols nvidia.ko may import with the owner that it does not
#                         import without it, separated by spaces; anything else refuses the owner
#   guest_ampere          the guest drivers for an Ampere card, as setup prints them
#   guest_ada             the guest drivers for an Ada card, as setup prints them
#
# Only host is required. A field that is left out is not recorded, and nothing is confirmed
# against it. A profile line is a claim that the combination was run on hardware. Leave it out
# for a combination with no recorded run: that combination is then built as unconfirmed.

# 580.159.01. Input identities from P0-MERGED-RECIPE, P0-HOST-CUDA and the verified idx39 output.
# display-cuda-580.159.01-idx39 rests on the saved output of one hardware run:
# evidence-e2e/24-cuda-evidence.txt (SHA-256
# 4f5d29a9349515705a484055f258bc73304823d615d2cb1fa051a1961016ab8e), boot 69d4b219, with the
# console on the boot display GPU, four vGPUs, and CUDA tests that passed on the host and in three
# guests. It is provisional until the same file is captured again on the build that follows the
# 0.3.0 refactor; remove the line if that run does not reproduce the result. Host CUDA without
# display has no recorded run and no profile.
